#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""thestill-aws — provision and manage the single-EC2 deployment (spec #66).

Mirrors the shape of dalston's ``dalston-aws``: a single-file boto3 CLI
rather than Terraform/CDK, split into a cheap idempotent ``setup`` phase and
a billable ``launch`` phase, so re-running ``setup`` is always safe.

Usage:
    thestill-aws setup --domain D [--dalston-sg sg-...] [--dalston-port P]
    thestill-aws secrets put --from-env-file FILE     # seed SSM from a .env
    thestill-aws secrets list                          # names only, never values
    thestill-aws launch                                # EC2 + cloud-init bootstrap
    thestill-aws dns                                   # Route53 A record -> instance
    thestill-aws reconcile [--image-tag T]             # re-apply on-box config / upgrade
    thestill-aws status                                # instance + bootstrap + health
    thestill-aws logs [--service thestill] [--lines N]
    thestill-aws ssh                                   # SSM Session Manager shell
    thestill-aws teardown [--yes]                      # delete everything but S3

Typical first deploy:
    thestill-aws setup --domain thestill.example.com --dalston-sg sg-0abc
    thestill-aws secrets put --from-env-file prod-secrets.env
    thestill-aws launch
    thestill-aws dns
    thestill-aws status

Prerequisites:
    - AWS credentials that can call sts:GetCallerIdentity
    - boto3 (already in thestill's [s3]/[dev] extras: ./venv/bin/python this-script)

Design notes:
    - Every command is idempotent. ``setup`` is get-or-create throughout;
      ``launch`` converges on one running instance (no-op if it is already
      up, start if stopped); ``dns`` skips a record that already matches;
      ``reconcile`` replays the same converging bootstrap over SSM. Re-run
      anything after a failure rather than unpicking partial state.
    - Access is SSM Session Manager only. No key pair, no port 22, ever.
    - IMDSv2 is required on the instance and the encrypted root mapping is
      resolved from the AMI's own RootDeviceName. Both are dalston M93
      findings (IMDSv1 left enabled; a hard-coded /dev/xvda mapping that
      silently produced an unencrypted root plus an idle encrypted volume).
    - EC2 tags are the source of truth for discovery; ~/.thestill/aws-state.json
      is a convenience cache. Deleting it does NOT delete AWS resources.
"""

